The attached image shows the physical characterization of Nano-11 nanoparticles. The left panel displays a Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM) image revealing spherical particles with an irregular, cauliflower-like surface, with the scale bar representing 50 nm. The right panel presents a histogram of particle size distribution, determined by dynamic light scattering, indicating a relatively uniform diameter of Nano-11 predominantly ranging between 70 and 80 nm. This characterization confirms the nanoscale dimensions and morphology of the alpha-D-glucan nanoparticles.

Nano-11, as a vaccine adjuvant, holds significant potential for cancer research, particularly in the development of therapeutic cancer vaccines. Its ability to enhance antigen uptake by dendritic cells (DCs), promote DC activation (increased expression of costimulatory molecules like CD80 and CD86, and secretion of IL-1β), and facilitate antigen delivery to migratory DCs in lymph nodes can be leveraged to induce potent anti-tumor immune responses. These properties are crucial for designing effective cancer vaccines that stimulate the immune system to recognize and eliminate cancer cells, with its good safety profile minimizing adverse effects.